<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE DECISIONS A DEBT-REPAYMENT LOOP RUNS UNDER — sprag's own, and nobody
  else's.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. `ai_loop.scxml` is a TEMPLATE: other repositories
  copy it, and the whole of what makes a run a DEBT run rather than a feature
  run is the data below. Until this file existed those clauses lived in the
  template, which meant sprag's standing yesses authorised every run of it, for
  everybody, until somebody edited the file. That is the hazard
  `a_template_names_nothing_of_this_repository_and_decides_nothing_for_the_next_one`
  was written to measure, and this file is what turns that gate green.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY IT IS A DOCUMENT AND NOT A RUST CONSTANT. The owner's rule: EVERY
  DECISION IS IN AN `.scxml`. A consent is a person having decided in advance,
  in writing; moving it into Rust would move it out of the reach of the person
  whose decision it is. The template still declares each id and still assigns
  it unconditionally at `start` — this file is where the VALUE comes from, and
  the driver only carries it across.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY IT IS A SIBLING AND NOT A PARENT. The first design had this file
  `<invoke>`ing the template and filling it with `<param>`. MEASURED and
  refuted: at the pinned SCE a generated parent owns its child as a PRIVATE
  field with no accessor, so a driver cannot read, pump or send to an invoked
  child (`error[E0616]: field 'child_probe' ... is private`, recorded in
  `probe.rs`). An invoked `ai_loop` would be undriveable. So the two documents
  stand side by side: the driver holds the template, reads this, and hands the
  values over at `start`.

  ⚠⚠ AND THAT IS THE SHAPE THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY CHOSE ONCE. `context_review`
  argues at length that an analysis with steps must be a machine rather than a
  function, and says in its own header that the loop will `<invoke>` it. What
  was actually built is `ContextReview` in `review.rs` — a second engine the
  driver drives directly, side by side. Nesting was the intent; siblings are
  what works.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠ WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It is not a second topology. It has one state and
  no transitions, because a KIND is a datamodel: what separates a debt loop
  from a feature loop is consents, standing instructions and prompt wording,
  never the order of the states. The day a kind needs STEPS of its own — an
  analysis before the first prompt, a judge to consult, a person to wait for —
  it becomes a machine the driver drives to completion first, which is what
  `ContextReview` already does. Nothing about that day requires this file to
  have been a parent.

  ⚠ SO THE STATE IS FINAL ON ENTRY. Nobody steps this machine; the driver
  initialises it and reads the datamodel, exactly as it reads the template's
  own authored strings before a run starts.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="debt_loop"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="decided">

  <datamodel>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH DIALOGS THIS LOOP MAY ANSWER *YES* TO, and with which option.
      The only thing in this repository that can put a byte into a permission
      dialog.

      Each clause is `{ asked, answer }`: text the QUESTION must carry for the
      clause to be about it, and text the OPTION must carry for the clause to
      authorise it.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE COST OF WRITING ONE HERE IS THAT IT IS COMMITTED. A clause
      authorises every debt run until somebody edits this file — where a run
      argument authorised one run. A standing yes is a different kind of
      promise from a standing think-about-it-again. What it now does NOT do is
      authorise runs in repositories that merely copied the template, which is
      the whole reason these two clauses moved here.

      ⚠⚠⚠ BOTH, BECAUSE EITHER ALONE IS A LOOP THAT CANNOT WORK. A run allowed
      to edit but not to run commands cannot test what it edited, and a
      milestone this loop calls reached is one it has verified. One consent is
      not half a working loop, it is a loop that stops at the other dialog.

      ⚠⚠ NEITHER `answer` NEEDLE IS `Yes`, measured, and the reason is the same
      both times: the edit dialog offers `1. Yes` / `2. Yes, allow all edits
      during this session` and the command dialog offers `1. Yes` / `2. Yes,
      and do not ask again` / `3. No, and tell me what to do`. `Yes` is carried
      by two options in each, so it names neither — `Consents` answers nothing
      at all when a needle reaches two. Each needle below is text only ONE
      option carries.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND A NEEDLE CAN GO STALE — THE EDIT ONE DID, AND IT COST FIVE HOURS
      AND TWENTY MINUTES (register item 525). This clause used to name `allow all
      edits during this session`, which was the second option's wording when it
      was written and is carried by NO option in the current `claude`: that
      button now reads `Yes, and switch to accept edits (auto-approve file edits
      and common file commands) for this session`. A needle that reaches ZERO
      options is the same as no clause at all, and an unattended run met the
      dialog on its first edit and stood there.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE REPAIR IS NOT THE STRING, IT IS THE GATE. `kind.rs`'s
      `every_clause_the_kind_authors_resolves_on_a_dialog_this_agent_really_draws`
      now resolves EVERY clause here against a dialog captured from a real agent
      (`testing::CLAUDE_EDIT_DIALOG_NOW`, taken 2026-08-21), so the next wording
      change is a red test rather than a run that stops at 3am. ⚠ The needle
      quotes the HEAD of that option because the label wraps across two rows on a
      100-column pane, and the tail sits past the fold.
    -->
    <data id="may_answer"
          expr="[
            { asked: 'Do you want to make this edit',
              answer: 'switch to accept edits' },
            { asked: 'Bash command',
              answer: 'do not ask again' }
          ]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THIS LOOP TURNS DOWN, AND WHAT IT SAYS INSTEAD. `may_answer`'s
      opposite number, and the weaker of the two by construction: the act's
      first key is a REFUSAL, so a rule can never GRANT anything. The failure it
      can cause is an agent told to reconsider too often, never one that did
      something nobody allowed.

      ⚠⚠ THE REPLY IS IN THE OWNER'S OWN LANGUAGE, and that is a reason this
      clause belongs here rather than in a template: it is said to somebody's
      agent, and which somebody is exactly what a template does not know.

      ⚠ IT QUOTES `create`, NOT THE WHOLE `Do you want to` FAMILY — a widening
      that was tried and MEASURED WRONG. The bare family needle swallows the
      COMMAND dialog (`Bash command ... Do you want to proceed?`), so a loop
      carrying it would be refused every `cargo test` it asked for and told to
      think again: a loop that may edit but may not run cannot verify a
      milestone it claims to have reached.
    -->
    <data id="screen_rules"
          expr="[
            { when: 'Do you want to create',
              text: '비용 무시하고 가장 장기적으로 올바른 방법으로 다시 생각하고 진행' }
          ]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠ WHAT MAKES A DIALOG ONE THIS LOOP JUDGES rather than quotes — EMPTY,
      and that is a decision rather than an omission.

      A judged rule costs a model call per blocked turn, and no criterion has
      been written that a debt run needs. The empty list declines it entirely:
      no judge is asked, nothing is paid for, and every blocked turn goes to
      `screening` exactly as it did before judged rules existed.

      ⚠ It is DECLARED rather than absent so that turning it on is editing a
      value here, in the file that owns this loop's decisions, rather than
      discovering which of two documents the id belongs to.
    -->
    <data id="judged_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS LOOP DOES NOT END ON TURNS, AND THAT IS THIS REPOSITORY'S
      DECISION RATHER THAN THE TEMPLATE'S.

      A debt run's job is a list nobody has finished, so a turn count is not what
      it is bounded by. It ends where a debt run actually ends: the north star
      declared, a person standing it down, a guardrail, or a peer that has gone.

      ⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED, AND IT IS WHY THE TURN CAP WAS NEVER THE RIGHT CEILING. Two
      runs ended `exhausted (turns)` mid-milestone, and what the document said
      about it was true and useless — *the plugin's own declared budget is spent*
      is a sentence about a number, never about the work. A run cut at a count
      leaves its milestone half-paid and its successor re-deriving where it got
      to; item 352 registers what that costs.

      ⚠⚠ THE WORD, NOT A BLANK OR A ZERO. `probe_absent.scxml` measured all three
      spellings: an id declared and left empty reads `Null`, and so does an id
      NOBODY DECLARED — so a kind that forgot this key would be granted an
      unbounded run. A boolean has the same disease (absent and `false` are both
      falsy). Only a value that is neither a number nor nil can carry a decision
      between documents.

      ⚠ AND IT IS NOT AN UNBOUNDED RUN. `max_iterations` and `max_seconds` still
      bound it in the substrate's own units; what is gone is a ceiling denominated
      in something the bill is not.
    -->
    <data id="max_turns" expr="'never'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW OFTEN THIS LOOP STOPS TO IMPROVE ITSELF — FIVE TURNS, AND IT MUST
      BE SAID HERE BECAUSE THE BUDGET ABOVE IS DECLINED.

      The template defaults this to `max_turns`, which is *never reflect on the
      budget* spelled as the one number that makes the guard unreachable. With no
      budget there is no such number, so a kind that declined one and said nothing
      here would ask for a loop that runs for ever and never once improves itself.
      The driver refuses that pairing rather than guessing; this is the answer.

      ⚠⚠⚠ FIVE IS AN IMPROVEMENT CADENCE. It is chosen on the axis
      `reflect_every` is actually for, and the numbers travel in the reflection
      prompt so the loop can weigh its own cadence rather than obey it blindly.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO OPEN *"AND KNOWN TO LOSE ON COST"* AND THE
      ARITHMETIC BEHIND THAT WAS SOMEBODY ELSE'S WORKLOAD — register item 424(a),
      which is exactly this defect: an override that carried no arithmetic of its
      own. It read *"real work here accumulates about 2,342 per turn, so five
      turns discard about 11,700 — BELOW the band"*, and the 2,342 is the
      TEMPLATE's figure, measured across a mixed population and inherited here
      without being re-run against the runs this file actually starts.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ RE-RUN, 2026-08-18, over 214 sessions of this repository's own agent
      with 20 billed requests or more: the discardable part grows **72,212 tokens
      per TURN at the median** and 120,928 at p75 — thirty-one times the rate the
      sentence assumed. Five turns discard about **361,000**, so this workload is
      not below the 15,000-21,000 band, it is more than an order of magnitude
      ABOVE it: 211 of those 214 sessions are above the band after five turns and
      not one is inside it. **The old sentence was wrong in both directions at
      once** — wrong that the arithmetic had been done here, and wrong about which
      side of the band the answer falls on.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT THAT CHANGES ABOUT THIS NUMBER IS NOTHING, AND SAYING SO IS THE
      POINT. A cadence is still not a cost knob, and the cost the old sentence
      named is now decided where it belongs: the template's `reviewing` replaces a
      session once `context - floor` reaches twenty times `cold`, on quantities
      each run measures about itself. Five is what it always claimed to be —
      how often this loop stops to choose — and no longer stands on an inherited
      sum that was never about it.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_every" expr="5"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW MANY TIMES IN A ROW THIS REPOSITORY'S CHECK MAY REFUSE A CLAIM
      before the run stops buying turns and reflects. TWO, where the template
      ships three — and this line exists at all because register item 494 built
      the road the template's own sentence had been promising.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE ARGUMENT IS THE TEMPLATE'S OWN, TAKEN UP RATHER THAN INVENTED.
      Item 449 chose three while refusals were MUTE: the agent was not told it
      had been refused at all, so three was generous — three turns spent
      answering a question nobody had asked. Item 448 gave every refusal the
      check's own words, and the template draws the consequence itself: *«an
      agent that fixes it on the second attempt is the LIKELY case rather than
      the hopeful one, and three refusals is three INFORMED attempts … a kind
      that finds it slack now has a fact it did not have then.»* This is a kind
      that finds it slack, saying so where a kind can.

      ⚠⚠ AND IT IS THIS REPOSITORY'S CHECK THAT MAKES IT SLACK. `milestone_check`
      above is a whole fresh `claude -p` per claimed milestone; a third informed
      attempt at persuading it costs another turn of the working agent AND
      another checker session, to test a hypothesis the second attempt already
      tested. Two keeps item 428's whole purpose — a refusal still buys one more
      turn — and bounds it one attempt earlier than a document that cannot know
      what its checker costs.

      ⚠ NOT ZERO OR ONE. Those reflect on the FIRST refusal, which the template
      allows and does not recommend, and which would spend a session replacement
      on the case an agent fixes by reading the check's sentence.

      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED 2026-08-27, AND UNTIL THAT DAY THE PARAGRAPHS ABOVE WERE
      THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS SAYS TWO. Register item 499 was filed against this
      line the hour it was written, for exactly that: **the argument is borrowed
      and nothing counts what it is about.** The reading it asked for is *how
      often a claim is refused twice and then believed on the third*, because
      that is the only case where two costs a session replacement three would
      have converged through.

      **It has never happened once.** Across the four unattended loops this
      daemon drives, 2026-08-20 to 2026-08-27, **492 claimed milestones and 32
      refusals, and every refusal stood alone.** Read two ways that fail
      differently, which is why both were taken:

        the agents' own transcripts   the sentence `disputing` composes is
                                      recorded verbatim in every session it was
                                      typed into, so a refusal cannot go missing;
                                      32 of them, and no session holds two with
                                      nothing between
        the runs' own walks           35 runs, 1,916 judgements: 4 refusals, and
                                      `reflecting` was entered 314 times carrying
                                      `milestone` (138), `budget` (173) and
                                      `capacity` (3). **`refused`: zero.** The
                                      ceiling this number sets has never fired.

      ⚠⚠ THE WALKS DROP LINES AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN WAVED AT: over the
      window both channels cover, the transcripts hold 5 refusals and the walks
      recorded 4, so that log lost one in five of exactly the transition item 706
      says it loses. It does not touch the finding. A lost `disputing` line is a
      lost FIRST refusal, and a first refusal is the case this number is not
      about.

      ⇒ **THE TEMPLATE'S THREE WAS SLACK AND THIS TWO IS SLACK TOO**, and that
      is what the item asked to be able to say. Neither bound has ever been
      reached, so nothing has been spent on the difference between them and the
      tighter one is free. What it still buys is the failure it was written for:
      run 16's NINE refusals in one session, which ended because a person pressed
      Escape.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE NUMBER IS NO LONGER READ OFF A TRANSCRIPT ARCHAEOLOGY. Item
      499's other half was that nothing in the workspace counted this, so every
      round that wanted to know had to reconstruct it from files that expire.
      A run now says it itself: `Checks::refused` and `Checks::refused_in_a_row`
      travel with `asked` and `silent`, the depth read from THIS document's own
      `refusals` so the driver never re-decides where a streak ends, and
      `sprag runs` prints it. **The next round asks the product.**

      ⚠ WHAT THE MEASUREMENT DOES NOT SAY: 32 refusals with no streak bounds the
      true rate of streaks near 9% rather than at zero. It is a reading, not a
      proof, which is the whole reason the channel above matters more than the
      number in this comment.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_after_refusals" expr="2"/>

    <!--
      🎯🎯🎯🎯🎯 HOW FAR A DEBT RUN MAY RE-AIM ITSELF AWAY FROM THE CHECKPOINT A
      PERSON GAVE IT — the owner's decision of 2026-09-02, verbatim: *"부채의
      부채는 몇 depth까지 갚을지 scxml에 지정할수있게하고 default로 1 depth로해"*.
      Register item 833(2).

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ IT IS AUTHORED HERE AND NOT LEFT TO THE TEMPLATE, even though the
      number is the same, and the reason is that THIS is the document a person
      tunes. The template's `1` is what a repository copying it starts from; this
      line is sprag's own answer, and a round that wants to try `2` for a week
      changes it here without touching a file every other repository shares.

      WHAT ONE MEANS, in this loop's own terms. Depth 0 is the register item the
      milestone names. Depth 1 is what the run finds WHILE PAYING that item —
      the residue that only exists because somebody went and looked. Depth 2 is
      what that in turn uncovers. A cap of one pays the first two and REGISTERS
      the rest, which is the whole of the owner's instruction.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ AND THE COUNT IS OF ANCESTORS STILL OWED, WHICH IS WHY A DEFERRAL
      ENDS — register item 921. Read the paragraph above once more with its own
      verb in mind: *what the run finds WHILE PAYING that item*. **A parent that
      has been paid is not being paid by anybody**, so it stops holding what it
      once found — 부채의 부채 is a debt whose parent is a DEBT, and a closed
      parent is not one. An item's causal depth never moves; what the cap counts
      falls as the chain above it closes.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WITHOUT THAT, THE NUMBER BOUNDED NOTHING IT COULD EVER RELEASE. The
      register's own words for a deferral were *not to be worked until the budget
      allows*, and nothing ever allowed: a chain is fixed once written, so a
      depth above the cap was permanent. Measured 2026-09-06: five items were
      being held under ancestors paid days earlier — one of them the loop's own
      `@sev: critical` item, standing since 09-03 — which is the exact inversion
      the register warns about in as many words, *the oldest debts sink fastest*.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔ AN UNMARKED PARENT STILL HOLDS. *Nobody said* is not *closed*, and
      the register carries hundreds of items that state no mark; letting them
      release their children would be an escape hatch that disables this cap
      quietly. Only a mark saying the parent has left the population stops the
      walk.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS ONE NUMBER FEEDS TWO BOUNDS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM CHANGED. The
      template's `reaimed` counts how many times THIS RUN has adopted a different
      milestone and starts at zero every run; the register counts the chain of
      the ITEM. `reflect.applied`'s guards compose them with OR on the REFUSAL,
      so a move needs BOTH to allow it. Register item 921 changed what the second
      one counts and left the first exactly as it was — so a person moving this
      number is still moving two things, and they no longer mean the same shape.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE MEASUREMENT THAT ASKED FOR IT, and it is this repository's own.
      2026-09-02, with no cap: twenty-two commits closed ELEVEN register items
      and NINE of the eleven had been registered that same day, while the
      forty-one items standing that morning lost exactly one. The population
      went UP, 41 to 50. Every one of those nine was real work; none of it was
      the work the run was launched for.

      ⚠⚠ THE RESIDUE THIS ACCEPTS, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. A capped run defers
      proposals instead of paying them, and a deferral nobody counts is
      indistinguishable from a run that found nothing. That is why the count is
      published on the run's row (`deferred`, and `sprag runs` prints it) and why
      the reflection prompt tells the agent to REGISTER what it set aside. If
      that number climbs while the register does not, this cap has become a way
      of losing findings and the right answer is a bigger number, not a quieter
      one.

      ⚠ `'never'` DECLINES THE CAP, spelled the way `max_turns` above spells it.
      What a kind may NOT do is say nothing: the driver refuses a document that
      declares no cap at all, because an absent policy that reads as *do what you
      like* is the escape hatch that disables its own gate.

      ═══ 🎯 ONE WAS RIGHT, AND HERE IS THE READING THAT SAYS SO — item 956 ⑴ ═══

      The clause above promised *"if that number climbs while the register does
      not, the right answer is a bigger number"*. Asked on 2026-09-08, over the
      EIGHTEEN runs that had a `successor_check` answer anything at all (runs 189
      195 199 205 210 217 218 220 227 230 232 237 242 245 248 250 253 257, from
      2026-09-03 on — the 227 earlier logs classify nothing, so no proposal of
      theirs could be a STEP and including them would only dilute the reading):

      ⚠ Written as a list and not a table because an XML comment may not carry a
      double hyphen, which is what a markdown rule row is made of.

      * milestones BANKED — `milestone: the agent said …` in the logs: **76**
      * STEP proposals REFUSED, all three refusal arms — `"STEP —` on such an
        edge: **77**
      * of those, the ones that ATE AN ASK — the same, counted only on the two
        arms a finished checkpoint reaches: **10**
      * DISTINCT register items this cap turned away — those, de-duplicated:
        **8** (480 795 808 856 865 939 943 951)
      * of those eight, STILL OPEN today — asked of the register per item: **4**
        (480 795 808 951)
      * runs that adopted MORE than one STEP — an adopting edge carrying
        `"STEP —`, de-duplicated per run: **0**

      ⇒ ⭐ **THE CAP BINDS AND IT IS NOT VACUOUS**: zero runs exceeded it in
      eighteen, and `north-star` prints `deferred 0 at depth > 1` off this very
      number. A bound nothing ever reaches would be a number to delete; this one
      is reached constantly and holds.

      ⇒ ⛔⛔⛔ **AND THE TWO RUNS IT WAS BLAMED FOR WERE NOT ITS FAULT.** Runs 248
      and 253 closed `unadmitted`, and in BOTH the whole ask-again budget had been
      spent on proposals the classifier ADMITTED — merit refusals ate an ask in
      neither. The cap had done exactly what it promises (*register it, the next
      run may take it*) and the ENDING WORD then forbade that remedy by naming the
      bad guess that arrived at the shut door. That is item 956 ⑵⑶ and it is
      repaired in the template (`reask_capped`), not here. ⚠ The harm was real and
      is measurable: 951 and 808 were the two proposals run 253 set aside, and
      both are still open.

      ⇒ ⛔⛔ **RAISING IT IS NOT FREE AND THE COST IS NOT IN THIS FILE.**
      `north-star` reads THIS declaration as the register's own deferral depth
      (its `DRIVING_DOCUMENT`), so a bigger number here does not only let a run go
      deeper — it changes which items the ledger admits at all. Item 659's cost,
      applied to the register rather than to one run.

      ⭐ **WHAT WOULD OVERTURN THIS, stated so a later round can just measure it**:
      the distinct-items-turned-away row climbing while the paid half of it does
      NOT — i.e. this cap deferring findings that no later run ever picks up. Four
      of eight paid is the reading that says deferral is working; four of twenty
      would say it is a way of losing findings, and then the answer is 2.
    -->
    <data id="reaim_max" expr="1"/>

    <!--
      🎯🎯🎯🎯🎯 AND HOW MANY WRONG GUESSES THIS REPOSITORY WILL SIT THROUGH when
      the cap above turns a proposal away and the checkpoint in hand is finished
      — the owner's decision of 2026-09-02, register item 840: *"다음에 즉시
      상환하도록해"*.

      ⛔ THE RUN USED TO END THERE, and that is what this number replaces. It could
      not move to the next debt either, because the budget counted CHANGES and
      moving is a change — so a mechanism built to make this loop go further was
      making it stop.

      📊📊📊📊📊 TWO, AND IT IS A READING NOW RATHER THAN AN ESTIMATE — register
      item 846. **What settles it is how often asking again LANDED**, and the
      answer is that the second ask does:

        ask     │ it was made │ the next proposal was ADOPTED
        ════════╪═════════════╪══════════════════════════════
        first   │     24      │  19   (79%)
        second  │      5      │   3   (60%)

      ⚠⚠ READ AT 2026-09-08T05:20:16Z over the 247 run logs this loop has kept
      since 2026-08-26, and THE MOMENT IS PART OF THE NUMBER — register item 918,
      whose rule `sprag-samples` already prints beside its own count: a total over
      a live file is true of an instant. This loop writes into that population
      WHILE it is being read, and a first draft of this table went stale between
      being measured and being committed.

      ⛔⛔ AND THE POPULATION IS RUNS THAT HAD A CLASSIFIER, because in the others
      a landing is IMPOSSIBLE rather than merely unobserved. With no
      `successor_check` the admission key is falsy, so nothing can ever answer
      *fresh*, and the template charges an unclassified movement as a step by the
      same rule 6 clause that guards `reaimed`. Once the depth budget is spent,
      every proposal is refused for ever.
      ⇒ Measured in the same breath: those runs made 15 ask-agains and landed
      ZERO, while adopting 22 proposals they were never made to ask twice for. So
      it is not a thin sample. Averaging them in reported *the second ask lands
      15%* by diluting a rate with a population that cannot contribute to it.

      ⇒ ⭐ **A BOUND OF ONE WOULD HAVE CLOSED THREE RUNS THAT RECOVERED** — runs
      189, 242 and 250, each turned away twice and then naming something its
      classifier admitted outright.
      ⇒ ⭐⭐ **AND THE COST IS COUNTED IN DEBTS RATHER THAN IN RUNS, because all
      THREE of those checkpoints were subsequently PAID**: items 870 (`43dfb55`),
      880 (`d19423e`) and 924 (`758ea6a`). Asked of the register per item, which
      is the standard `reaim_max` above holds itself to. So lowering this to one
      does not merely end three runs early — it is three entries of this
      repository's own debt that the second ask is what got retired.
      ⇒ ⛔⛔ **AND THERE IS NO READING THAT WOULD RAISE IT, because a third ask
      was never made.** A landing deeper than this bound is censored by the bound
      itself, so *the second one lands* is evidence FOR two and never for three.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE SENTENCE THIS REPLACES WAS WRONG TWICE, AND THE SECOND TIME
      WAS TODAY. Register item 956 had already found the first: *"every refusal
      raises `deferred`, so a later round can ask whether second guesses actually
      land"* — **`deferred` cannot answer it**, being a total over all THREE
      refusal arms, and the arm back to `working` never touches this budget (run
      237 set 42 proposals aside there while asking again ONCE). ⇒ Item 956 then
      nominated `reask_capped` in its place, and **that one cannot answer it
      either**: it counts asks that were EATEN and is RESET the moment a
      checkpoint is adopted, so all 22 landings above leave no trace on any
      stored row. The same defect, one level up, on the very number named as its
      repair. 🎯 The numbers that CAN answer it are `reask_landed` and
      `reask_landed_deepest`, published per run since item 846.

      ⛔ AND THE OLD DEFENCE — *"nobody has yet watched an agent guess twice
      here"* — WAS TRUE OF THE WRONG POPULATION. Narrowly it holds: no run ever
      spent two asks on proposals a classifier refused on merit. But this bound
      does not count merit refusals, it counts **every** refusal on that arm, and
      by the number it actually bounds a second refusal has happened 19 times.
      A defence has to be about the thing the guard reads.

      ⭐ WHAT WOULD OVERTURN THIS, stated so a later round can just measure it:
      `reask_landed_deepest` never reaching two across a season of runs — the
      second ask having stopped paying — and then the answer is one.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS NOT A DECLINE. Unbounded has no spelling in this slot, deliberately
      (see the template): an agent that keeps naming refused things would turn for
      ever. Zero would mean *never ask again*, which is the behaviour this item
      exists to end — and the reading above is what says zero is wrong: asking
      again bought 22 checkpoints that would otherwise have closed a run.
    -->
    <data id="reask_max" expr="2"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND WHO DECIDES A CHECKPOINT WAS REACHED — NOT THE AGENT THAT WORKED
      ON IT. Register item 428, whose mechanism the template shipped and whose
      second half is this line: the slot exists, EMPTY is the template's default,
      and empty means nobody checks. A loop that runs unattended for hours on the
      strength of its own agent writing `MILESTONE REACHED` is the exact failure
      the measured literature it cites priced — 92 of 100 runs recorded
      `succeeded`, 18 of them repairs of runs already recorded that way.

      A FRESH `claude`, given the checkpoint and what the turn produced and
      NOTHING about what the agent said of its own work, answering one word. That
      is the *"different agent, new session, shown only the artifact"* shape the
      item names, spelled in the two arguments this slot can carry: the driver
      appends the rendered question as the third.

      ⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS AUTHORED (2026-08-18), all three poles against a
      real `claude` 2.1.234, because a check that cannot refuse and a check that
      cannot agree are both worthless:

        an artifact that MEETS a five-sentence checkpoint  -> YES
        an artifact that misses it (one sentence)          -> NO
        an EMPTY artifact                                  -> NO

      ⚠⚠ THAT THIRD LINE IS WHY THIS COULD NOT BE AUTHORED UNTIL TODAY. The
      artifact used to be read off the PANE, and a full-screen agent's pane was
      measured unable to advance its line addresses at all (item 441) — so the
      checker would have been shown nothing, refused every milestone for ever,
      and bought one more turn each time until the run ran out. The driver asks
      the agent what it said now, and only falls back to the pane.

      ⚠ WHAT IT COSTS: one short `-p` session per CLAIMED milestone — not per
      turn. A checkpoint is claimed once, and a refusal buys one more turn rather
      than ending anything.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT ASKS FOR A STRUCTURED ANSWER, WHICH IS REGISTER ITEM 752's
      WHOLE MECHANISM. A checker stopped by a usage limit, a hook or an outage
      prints a notice and no verdict, and to a reader of the SCREEN that is
      indistinguishable from a checker that judged and phrased it badly. The two
      remedies are opposite.

      Matching the notice's wording is the widening `service_needles` refuses two
      hundred lines up, and this machine's transcripts hold the proof rather than
      the argument: a line whose head is the peer's usage notice and whose tail is
      an AGENT writing about it. So what tells them apart is a PROMISE instead —
      this argv asks for a shape, and an answer that is not that shape is the
      program reporting its own failure in its own words.

      ⚠⚠ A KIND THAT DROPS THIS FLAG LOSES THE THIRD CLASS AND KEEPS THE OTHER
      TWO — `judge::promised_shape` speaks only where the argv promised, so the
      fact becomes unavailable rather than guessed. That is the honest failure
      and it is why `unwell_rule` below is authored beside this line.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_check" expr="'claude -p --output-format json'"/>

    <!--
      🎯🎯🎯🎯🎯 AND WHO SAYS A PROPOSED CHECKPOINT IS ONE TO TAKE NEXT — register
      item 839, and the owner's decision of 2026-09-02: *"전부 공통되게 해야해,
      지금 프로젝트말고 다른 프로젝트에서도 자동으로 적용되게 범용적으로 들어가야해"*.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHAT THIS REPLACES IS A PARAGRAPH OF `working_rules` BELOW. Rules
      11 to 14 down there say what a round takes first — while anything is marked
      critical, take from those; a debt deeper than the re-aiming cap is
      registered rather than worked. They are PROSE. `working_rules` is one of six
      fragments concatenated into `start_prompt`; no `cond` reads it, no gate
      holds a run to it, and a run that ignored it was refused by nobody. The
      supervisor that wrote those four rules put them on the line under rule 10,
      which says exactly that. This is that decision made into a predicate.

      🎯🎯🎯🎯🎯 AND WHICH WAY THE FORK WENT — register item 833(3). Item 833's
      first clause left an open choice and its own ledger recorded that the
      answer was owed: rank the register BY SEVERITY, or make severity READABLE
      and show what a round passed over. Item 659 had already argued against the
      first, and the argument is kept rather than overruled — a loop that always
      chases the sharpest thing never finishes an axis, and items that share a
      seam are cheap exactly because they are worked together.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ SO THE ANSWER IS NEITHER: IT IS A GATE AND NOT A SORT. The
      predicate below answers *may this be taken* and never *what to take
      first*, so a round is free to work a seam in whatever order coheres as
      long as every item it takes is in the admissible set. `Reading::admits`
      states the same thing where it is implemented, and the two are held
      together by `the_rules_and_the_predicate_name_one_authority`.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT MAKES THIS A RECORD RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE is that the choice is
      visible in the SHAPE: a sort would have to answer with an ordered list and
      this answers with a set and a yes/no. A later reader who wants the other
      branch has to change the shape, not just the wording.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE RULE ITSELF IS NOT HERE, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. `ai_loop.scxml`
      holds the machine — count a proposal you may not take, and do not take it —
      and other repositories copy that file, so it may not know the word
      `critical` or that this tree keeps a register at all. What may be taken here
      is `north-star`'s admissibility mode, which composes this repository's own
      two lists (`critical` while it is non-empty, else the population the depth
      cap allows) and answers YES or NO with one sentence.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS `milestone_check` ABOVE and deliberately not the
      same KIND of program. That one asks a model to read a tree; this asks a
      predicate to read a file, so it is deterministic, it costs nothing anybody
      is billed for, and it cannot be talked round. Measured the day it was
      written: 2.22 s from a cold build of its own binary, 0.27 s warm.

      ⚠⚠ ITS OWN TARGET DIRECTORY, WHICH IS NOT TIDINESS. `target/` in this tree
      is shared with the agent this loop is driving (register item 196), and a
      `cargo` invocation that queued behind that agent's build would outrun the
      driver's bound and answer *silent* — which sets every proposal aside. A
      separate directory cannot contend with it, and `sprag-gate` declares no
      dependencies, so what it costs on a cold cache is one crate.

      ⚠ THE MANIFEST IS NAMED ABSOLUTELY rather than left to the spawn's
      directory, on `reference` below's terms: this document is this repository's
      own, and a classifier that silently answered about whatever tree it happened
      to land in would be worse than one that failed to start.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE `locked` SWITCH IN THE ARGV BELOW IS THE ONE THING HERE PUT
      IN BY A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY A DECISION — register item 841.

      Without it this classifier WRITES THE TREE IT IS JUDGING. A `cargo run`
      re-resolves whenever a manifest has moved past `Cargo.lock`, and
      re-resolving writes: measured 2026-09-08 in a throwaway worktree, one path
      dependency added to one member was enough, and the classifier answered
      `YES STEP` and rewrote that lockfile in the same second. Register item 196
      is that this tree has TWO writers, so a lock rewritten under the agent by
      its own referee lands in whatever that agent commits next, unread.

      ⚠⚠ SO A STALE LOCK NOW COSTS THIS RUN ITS CLASSIFIER, AND THAT TRADE IS
      TAKEN DELIBERATELY: cargo refuses instead of writing, its first printed
      line says exactly why, and `Unheard::NotAVerdict` carries that line into
      the row as `Admits::Silent`. Silence sets a proposal aside and keeps the
      brief, which is the safe direction this whole document takes about a
      process that did not answer. A rewritten lockfile is not recoverable by
      keeping a brief.

      ⛔⛔⛔ AND WHAT THIS ARGV CLAIMS IS A PREDICATE RATHER THAN THIS PROSE —
      `sprag_gate::classifier`, driven by
      `the_classifier_the_loop_runs_compiles_no_product_crate`. It reads THIS
      line, builds what it names into an empty target directory, and refuses
      unless the only crate cargo compiles is the one named here. The four
      measurements behind that, the register's own disproven headline included,
      are in the module's documentation.
    -->
    <data id="successor_check"
          expr="'env CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/coin/.cache/sprag-admits ' +
                'cargo run -q --locked ' +
                '--manifest-path /home/coin/sprag/Cargo.toml ' +
                '-p sprag-gate --bin north-star -- --admits ' +
                '/home/coin/.claude/projects/-home-coin-sprag/memory/debt-open.md'"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ AND WHAT THIS REGISTER SAYS TO TAKE NEXT — register item 659, the
      clause above's other half, and the SAME PROGRAM asked its other question.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE DEFECT IT PAYS: this loop has been scored by this instrument on
      every reflection turn and never shown what it said. The classifier above
      answers *may this proposal be taken*; this mode answers *what is first*,
      derived from the same reading — and until item 659 the only way that
      answer reached a round was inside a REFUSAL, which is a round trip late
      and only when the answer is no.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE SAME TARGET DIRECTORY as the clause above, and for its reason:
      register item 196, this tree has two writers, and a `cargo run` that built
      into the shared `target/` would relink under the round that is working in
      it. Two invocations one turn apart share the compile; the second is a
      process start and a file read.

      ⚠⚠ MEASURED before it was wired, with this exact argv: 0.32 s and 0.41 s
      warm, against a turn's own bound of 1 800 000 ms — 0.023%. The number that
      made this look expensive was `ADMITS_WITHIN` (120 s), which is the WAIT a
      classifier nobody here has seen is allowed, not what this program costs.

      ⚠ THE MODE NAMED BELOW PRINTS THE SENTENCE ALONE and puts every failure on
      stderr, so the driver takes the last line and quotes it only where the
      program reported success. The plain report would put twenty lines of
      population counts into a live prompt; its own doc (`fn next` in
      `north-star.rs`) carries why the mouth exists.
    -->
    <data id="order_check"
          expr="'env CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/coin/.cache/sprag-admits ' +
                'cargo run -q --locked ' +
                '--manifest-path /home/coin/sprag/Cargo.toml ' +
                '-p sprag-gate --bin north-star -- --next ' +
                '/home/coin/.claude/projects/-home-coin-sprag/memory/debt-open.md'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE WORDS THIS REPOSITORY'S PEER PRINTS WHEN ITS SERVICE FAILS, and
      the template ships this empty for exactly the reason this file can fill it:
      the sentence is `claude`'s, and this document is the one that knows its
      runs talk to `claude`.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ QUOTED FROM A LIVE RUN, NOT INVENTED — the mistake this workspace has
      made before with needles. Run 15, pane 99, 2026-08-19, after 28m37s of
      work, verbatim on the screen:

          API Error: 529 Overloaded. This is a server-side issue, usually
          temporary — try again in a moment.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE NEEDLE IS THE SHORT HEAD OF IT, FOR TWO MEASURED REASONS. The
      message WRAPS — it arrived across two rows — and the screen is read the way
      `pane_collapsed` joins it, rows concatenated with no separator, so a needle
      that spans the wrap still matches while one carrying the whole sentence
      would depend on where the terminal happened to break it. And the tail is
      prose that has changed before; the code has not.

      ⚠⚠ IT DOES NOT QUOTE `API Error` ALONE, which was the widening considered
      and turned down here for `screen_rules`' own measured reason: a 400 is this
      run's fault and will still be its fault in ten minutes, and waiting one out
      forever is a worse ending than stopping. **A wait is the treatment for a
      SERVER's bad minute, and 529 is the code that means that one.** Another
      code that earns it is another element for whoever measures it.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SECOND AND THIRD ELEMENTS ARE THE INTERRUPTION THIS LOOP
      MEETS MOST — register item 715. A 529 is a bad minute upstream and RARE. A
      USAGE LIMIT is neither: it is the single most predictable stop in an
      unattended loop's life, it recurs, and **it resolves itself**. It reached
      nothing here, because a needle that is one string can only be one sentence,
      and the run died at `blocked` while the peer went on working.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHERE THESE TWO COME FROM, AND IT IS NOT A TRANSCRIPTION. They are the
      peer's OWN records: `claude` writes each of these notices into its session
      transcript, and twelve distinct ones were read out of this machine's
      `~/.claude/projects` on 2026-08-27 (2026-08-20 through 2026-08-27). Every
      one of the eleven OUTAGE notices reads

          Usage limit reached · continuing automatically at <time> · esc or type to cancel

      or the same head followed by `again after you continued`, and the twelfth is
      the RECOVERY notice, `Usage limit reset · continuing automatically`.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE TWO NEEDLES ARE CHOSEN AGAINST THE FAMILY, NOT AGAINST A
      SENTENCE, and each one is a fact rather than a wording:
        * `Usage limit reached` — the peer says the limit is what stopped it.
        * `continuing automatically at` — the peer says it is coming back, and
          when. **The trailing `at` is load-bearing**: without it this also
          matches `Usage limit reset · continuing automatically`, which is the
          peer telling us the outage is OVER. A needle that matched the recovery
          notice would file a working peer as a broken one.

      ⚠⚠ AND NEITHER IS THE BARE WORD `limit`, which is the widening this loop in
      particular must refuse: its own agent WRITES about usage limits — this
      comment is on that agent's screen right now — and `service_failed` falls
      back to reading the pane. A needle a run can print by working is a needle
      that stops the run for working.

      ⚠ BOTH ARE SHORT for the wrap reason two paragraphs up: 19 and 25
      characters, where the sentence they come from is 76.
    -->
    <data id="service_needles"
          expr="[
            { says: 'API Error: 529' },
            { says: 'Usage limit reached' },
            { says: 'continuing automatically at' }
          ]"/>

    <!--
      TEN MINUTES, THE OWNER'S NUMBER (2026-08-19), and the same as the
      template's default — written out rather than left implicit because a kind
      that names the needle should be readable as having chosen the whole
      behaviour, not two thirds of it.
    -->
    <data id="service_retry_ms" expr="600000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠ WHAT A PERSON TYPES AT THAT SCREEN, which is the whole design: the
      session survived the outage with its brief and its half-finished turn, so
      the smallest thing that resumes it is the word that means carry on. Asking
      the milestone again would spend the context the outage did not take.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS THE 529 DOOR'S WORD ONLY — register item 715. The limit
      notices above say `esc or type to cancel` in the same breath, so this word
      typed at THAT screen cancels the peer's own recovery. The template decides
      which door speaks (`service_resumes_itself`); this value is what is said
      when one does, and it stays here unchanged.
    -->
    <data id="service_retry_text" expr="'continue'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHY THE CADENCE ABOVE NO LONGER BUYS A SESSION REPLACEMENT, which is
      what let it go back to being only a cadence.

      `reflecting` asks what to do next. It used to be followed by `restarting`
      unconditionally, so an improvement cadence and a session-replacement
      policy were the same number wearing one name — and five was chosen on the
      first axis while paying on the second. The template now asks whether there
      is ROOM before it replaces anything, so five is what it always claimed to
      be: how often this loop stops to choose.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE CEILING IS FOR, AND WHY IT IS A NUMBER AT ALL WHERE THE
      TEMPLATE REFUSED ONE. The template's refusal was about ECONOMICS — is a
      restart worth its cold start — and it was right that a threshold inside the
      band decides by rounding. This is CAPACITY: a session's reading is
      bounded, and a bound near the end of the range is not a coin toss.

      ⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED HERE, 2026-08-18, on this loop's own inner session: ONE turn
      reached **277,933** tokens of cache read across 293 billed requests, with
      a floor of 22,124 — so `context - floor` was **255,809** after a single
      turn that produced three commits. Five such turns are not a policy anybody
      measured; they are whatever five turns happen to be.

      ⚠⚠ 800,000 IS CHOSEN AGAINST A 1M WINDOW and is stated rather than
      derived, because THE WINDOW IS NOT IN THIS DATAMODEL — the agent knows it
      and the loop does not. The residue that follows is named rather than
      hidden: against a SMALLER window this ceiling never fires and the loop
      behaves exactly as it did before, replacing on every reflection. That is
      the fail-safe direction — the old behaviour — and not a silent one.

      ⚠ What would replace the guess is the agent stating its own window, the
      same way it now states the prompt it was asked and the transcript it
      writes. Until then this is a number with a reason, which is the most this
      document can honestly hold.
    -->
    <data id="context_ceiling" expr="800000"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A DEBT RUN OWES AT ITS ENDING, BEYOND THE ACCOUNT EVERY RUN GIVES.
      Appended to the template's closing question through `closing_rules`.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE SWEEP IS THE POINT, AND IT IS NOT A COURTESY. This repository's own
      record says it twice over: *the sweep starts with «no» every time and every
      time it found something*, and *a recorded lesson is not an applied one*. A
      run that noticed a defect and neither fixed nor registered it has spent the
      finding — the next round pays to find it again, and this has happened often
      enough to be the reason this clause exists rather than a worry.

      ⚠⚠ PAY IT OR REGISTER IT, AND EITHER IS A COMPLETE ANSWER. Demanding the
      fix would make the honest reply *"I ran out of room"* impossible to give,
      and a run that hides an unfixed finding to look finished is the failure this
      is trying to prevent, not a version of success.
    -->
    <data id="closing_rules"
          expr="' Then sweep for debt this session turned up — anything you found and did not fix, and anything you learned that the next run would otherwise re-derive. Pay what you can pay now; register the rest, with what you measured. Say which you did for each.'"/>

    <!--
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHAT THIS KIND DOES ABOUT ITS OWN CHECKER'S SILENCE — register item
      741, and the two clauses are opposite remedies rather than one sentence
      said twice.

      This loop's checker is `claude -p` (`milestone_check` below), and what that
      program does when it cannot answer was MEASURED across this repository's
      entire run log — nineteen silences in twelve runs, six of those runs with
      nothing verified at all, and two runs that BANKED a milestone nothing had
      checked. By the arm the driver classed them under:

        Unfinished    4   the wait ended without an answer (`NotYet` ×3, `RunEnded`)
        NotAVerdict  15   it answered, and no word of the answer was YES or NO

      and by what those fifteen replies OPENED with: `Permission` ×3 — `You've`
      ×2, a usage-limit notice — `Trim`, a hook refusing it — and nine that are
      genuinely a verdict in the wrong shape (`Verdict`, `Gate`,
      `Counterfactual`, `Re-measured`, `The` ×2, `Two`, `Still`, and one empty
      reply).

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ AND REGISTER ITEM 743 DISPROVED THE READING OF THE LARGEST GROUP.
      `Permission` is not a checker sitting at a dialog: it is the opening of an
      ADVISORY the tool prints about a loose allow rule in the consuming
      repository's `.claude/settings.local.json`, and the judge's `YES` was on
      the line UNDER it. The driver now finds a marked verdict anywhere in the
      reply, so those three — and most of the nine — are READ. What is left in
      this class is `You've` and `Trim`: replies with no verdict in them at all.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ SO THE TWO CLAUSES ARE WRITTEN AGAINST THAT DISTRIBUTION AND NOT
      AGAINST A GUESS. `unanswered` is told to look at the ASKING, because that
      is where its four cases live; `unreadable` is told to make the next reply
      LEAD with the verdict, because a reply this driver cannot find one in is
      the only thing that still lands there.

      ⚠⚠ AND NEITHER SAYS *the milestone is wrong*. A silence is not a refusal —
      `judging`'s own block spells that out — and telling an agent its work was
      rejected because a permission dialog ate the checker would send it to
      re-do work nobody disputed. Both clauses ask about the CHECKER.

      ⚠ IN THE OWNER'S OWN LANGUAGE, `screen_rules`' reason exactly: it is said
      to somebody's agent, and which somebody is what a template cannot know.
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <data id="unanswered_rule"
          expr="'검사기가 판정을 «내지 못했다» — 물어본 쪽을 봐라: argv, 시작 실패, 시간 초과, 페인이 답을 셈하지 못함 중 하나다. 이 종류는 «다시 물으면» 답이 나올 수 있다. 마일스톤 주장은 그대로 두되, 다음 주장 전에 검사기가 실제로 답하는지 확인해라.'"/>
    <!--
      AND THE OTHER HALF. See the block above for the measurement both are
      written from.
    -->
    <data id="unreadable_rule"
          expr="'검사기가 답은 했는데 그 답 «어디에도» 판정 낱말이 없었다 — 다시 물어도 같은 모양이 온다. 고칠 것은 검사기의 프롬프트나 프로그램이다: 답이 YES 또는 NO 로 «시작»하게 만들어라(문장 안에 소문자로 묻힌 판정은 이 드라이버가 못 본다). 마일스톤 주장 자체가 거절된 것은 아니다.'"/>
    <!--
      AND THE THIRD — register item 752. It is the one an unattended loop meets
      most and the one the other two could not name: the checker RAN and never
      got as far as judging, because a usage limit or a hook stopped it. Its
      remedy is `unanswered_rule`'s (ask again) with a WAIT in front of it, which
      is why it could borrow neither spelling.
    -->
    <data id="unwell_rule"
          expr="'검사기가 «판정에 이르지도 못했다» — 형식을 약속한 argv 로 물었는데 그 형식이 안 왔다(사용 한도·훅·정전 중 하나다). 프롬프트를 고칠 일이 아니고 «즉시» 다시 물을 일도 아니다: 무엇이 검사기를 멈췄는지 확인하고, 그것이 풀린 뒤에 다시 물어라. 마일스톤 주장은 거절된 것이 아니라 «아직 검증되지 않은» 것이다.'"/>

    <!--
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE THREE CLAUSES BELOW ARE, AND WHY THEY ARRIVED LATE — register
      item 738, layers 2 and 3.

      Everything above this line is a decision about HOW a debt run behaves. What
      was still missing is the plainer half: WHAT THE RUN IS FOR, and WHAT MAKES
      ITS PEER READY TO BE DRIVEN. Neither was written anywhere a build can read.
      They were typed by hand into the launch arguments of every single firing —
      out of one person's memory, and out of one session's context.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY THIS IS A DEFECT RATHER THAN AN
      INCONVENIENCE. Of the thirteen values a caller handed over on every launch,
      exactly THREE were genuinely about that call (`pane`, `agent`, `plugin`);
      the other ten were the same values every time, because they are properties
      of the KIND. `ready_when` was `{ settles, claude }` on this repository's runs
      and on another repository's run 39 alike. A value that never varies and lives
      only in somebody's memory is not a run argument — it is an unwritten
      decision, and the session holding it dies at the end of the day.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE SHAPE IS `closing_rules`', DELIBERATELY. That clause is this file's
      own precedent for putting PROSE A RUN OBEYS into a document: the template
      declares the id and ships it empty, this file holds the value, `LoopKind`
      reads it, the door falls through to it when no caller said otherwise, and the
      template assigns it at `start`. Nothing new is invented for these; the road
      that carries the sweep carries them.

      ⛔ AND THE WRONG REPAIR IS NAMED HERE SO IT IS NOT REACHED FOR AGAIN: a guard
      chain in the launcher script. That is where these values lived, in an
      untracked directory four supervisors share, and the owner's judgement of it is
      the reason this item exists — a copy of somebody's memory is not a spec.
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE RULES EVERY SESSION OF THIS KIND WORKS UNDER — the standing orders
      a debt run is held to whoever drives it and whichever milestone it is on.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THEY ARE NOT CALLED `standing_rules`, WHICH IS WHAT A PERSON CALLS
      THEM. The template already owns `standing`, and that is a DIFFERENT thing with
      a different lifetime: it is empty until a `screen_rules` clause FIRES, and it
      accumulates what a run was redirected to mid-flight. Two ids one word apart,
      one authored and one accumulated, is how a reader comes to believe a fired
      instruction and an author's rule are the same channel. The word that separates
      them is what a rule is FOR — these are worked under, that one was said.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ IT GOES INTO `start_prompt` AND NOWHERE ELSE, WHICH IS A COST DECISION
      TAKEN ON PURPOSE. `start_prompt` is what every session of a run is greeted
      with — the first one and every replacement `restarting` opens — so a rule
      written here reaches every agent that ever works for this kind. Putting it in
      `turn_prompt` as well would retype the whole block on every turn of a run that
      ends on its work rather than on a count, for an agent whose session already
      holds it.
      ⚠ The residue, stated rather than hidden: a session many turns in has these
      rules only at the top of its context. What restates them is the replacement
      `reviewing` and `reflecting` buy — the same channel that restates the
      milestone — and not a repetition on every turn.

      ⚠⚠ AND THERE IS NO WIRE KEY FOR IT, on `closing_rules`' own argument: what a
      repository holds its runs to is its document's business, and a caller who
      could override it could delete it by naming nothing. A caller with something
      to add adds it to the milestone, which is theirs.
    -->
    <data id="working_rules"
          expr="'상시 규칙, 전부 지켜라:\n' +
                '(1) 한글로 작업하고 보고한다.\n' +
                '(2) 비용 무시 — 언제나 가장 장기적으로 올바른 선택. 가장 간단한 방법으로 때우지 말 것.\n' +
                '(3) 마일스톤을 주장하는 턴은 그 턴 안에서 증거를 다시 만들어라.\n' +
                '(4) 착수하면 원장의 문장을 명령으로 다시 재라: 전제·숫자·처방 전부. 재측정이 원장을 반증하는 것은 예외가 아니라 기대값이다.\n' +
                '(5) 종료조건을 세는 술어에 「이 값이 0 이 되는 경로가 실제로 있는가」를 물어라. 없으면 모집단에 원래 0 이 될 수 없는 역할이 섞여 있다.\n' +
                '(6) 탈출구(면제 목록·기본값·「해당 없음」)가 제 게이트를 무력화하지 않게 하라 — 분류되지 않은 것은 통과가 아니라 RED 다.\n' +
                '(7) 게이트는 뮤테이션으로 red 를 보여야 산다.\n' +
                '(8) 라운드 종료 = 커밋 + 원장 갱신 + push. 푸시 판정 SSOT 는 git ls-remote origin main.\n' +
                '(9) git add -A 금지 — 트리를 다른 세션과 공유한다.\n' +
                '(10) 산문으로 적힌 근거는 아무도 재지 않는다 — 근거는 문서나 생성물에 물어 확인되는 술어로 만들어라.\n' +
                '(11) 무엇을 다음에 할지는 «고르지» 말고 계기에 물어라: north-star 가 내는 critical 줄이 비어 있지 않으면 거기서 take 한다. 비어 있으면 population 을 이음매가 맞는 순서로 일한다. ⛔ 「가장 날카로워 보이는 것」을 눈으로 고르지 마라 — 그 판단이 어디에도 안 남는다.\n' +
                '(12) unranked 는 «보통»이 아니라 «아무도 안 정한 것»이다. 그 수는 줄기만 하고 늘 수 없다(래칫) — 항목을 새로 열면 그 자리에서 critical 인지 ordinary 인지 적어라.\n' +
                '(13) 항목을 새로 열 때 «무엇이 그것을 만들었는지» 적어라: 지금 갚는 항목이 그것을 만들었으면 `@from: 그 번호`, 이미 있던 것을 «마주친» 것이면 `@from: none`. ⛔ 「갚다가 생긴 것」과 「갚다가 마주친 것」은 다르다 — 후자를 자식으로 적으면 남이 만든 red 를 밟을 때마다 사슬이 길어지고, 오래된 부채일수록 빨리 가라앉는다.\n' +
                '(14) 그 사슬의 깊이가 문서의 재조준 상한을 넘으면 그 항목은 «등록만 하고 이번에 갚지 마라». 그 상한이 «몇»인지는 여기 안 적는다 — north-star 의 deferred 줄이 「at depth > N」으로 그 문서의 값을 그대로 낸다. 0 이 아니면 그것은 미납이 아니라 «미룬 것»이고, 상한이 풀릴 때 첫 후보다.\n'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A SESSION OF THIS KIND CARRIES BESIDE ITS MILESTONE, when the
      caller names nothing — the LEDGER, which is the only prior art a debt run
      ever starts from.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TEMPLATE SHIPS `(edit me)` HERE, and that placeholder has reached a
      live agent before (R380 measured three of five clauses arriving as `(edit
      me)`). So the fall-through cannot be to the template: a caller who says
      nothing must get THIS repository's ledger, and a launch with neither is
      refused at the door naming the key, rather than briefing an agent with an
      instruction to edit a file.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS STILL OVERRIDABLE, unlike `working_rules` above, and the asymmetry
      is the point. The rules are what this repository holds its runs to; the
      reference is what a session should read FIRST, and a reflection rewrites it
      every time it replaces a session — *what the last session had to work out*.
      A value a reflection may rewrite mid-run is not one this document can own
      outright; what it can own is where a run that has learnt nothing yet starts.

      ⚠ THE PATH IS ABSOLUTE AND OUTSIDE THIS REPOSITORY, which is stated rather
      than hidden: the register lives in the agent's memory directory, not in
      version control. That is items 655 and 627, already registered, and naming it
      here does not make it worse — it moves ONE copy of the address out of a
      person's head and into a file a build reads.
    -->
    <data id="reference"
          expr="'/home/coin/.claude/projects/-home-coin-sprag/memory/MEMORY.md 와 같은 폴더의 debt-open.md — 색인이 먼저이고, 항목의 표제·done-when·실측은 전부 원장에 있다. ⚠ 원장의 문장은 늙는다: 착수하면 전제·숫자·처방을 제품에 다시 물어라.'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT MAKES A PANE READY FOR THIS KIND'S FIRST PROMPT — and the one
      clause here that does NOT travel through the template's datamodel.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY IT IS NOT A `<data>` IN `ai_loop.scxml` LIKE THE OTHER TWELVE. Item
      300 measured the line: what makes a pane ready is a PREDICATE ABOUT THE PEER
      and is the same for every run against it, while how long anybody waits for it
      is a judgement. Predicates live on the run spec, judgements live in the
      datamodel — and the type is what enforces it, so a barrier cannot be spelled
      as a `<data>` without undoing that. This clause changes WHO AUTHORS the
      predicate, not which side of the line it is on.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS NOT A SECOND AUTHOR OF THE AGENT'S NAME, WHICH IS THE THING
      TO GET RIGHT HERE. A caller who names an `agent` still has the barrier DERIVED
      from it, exactly as before — this value is only what a launch that named no
      agent gets. So the order is: what the caller spelled, then what the caller
      implied by naming a program, then this. A run driving `codex` gets `codex`,
      because the caller said so and a caller saying so is more specific than a
      document's standing default.

      ⚠⚠ THIS DOCUMENT ALREADY COMMITTED TO THIS PEER, which is why it may hold
      this at all: `service_needles` above quotes sentences only `claude` prints,
      and `milestone_check` spells `claude -p`. A kind that knows what its peer says
      when it is unwell is a kind that knows which peer it has.

      ⚠ `settles`, not `prints` or `shows`: it is the one barrier word an agent that
      prints nothing on startup can be waited for by, it asks the operating system
      rather than the screen, and no amount of typing the word can satisfy it. R379
      measured the cost of no barrier at all — a first prompt typed into a pane
      whose agent had existed for ten milliseconds, and a run that then sat in
      `working` for as long as anybody let it.
    -->
    <data id="ready_when"
          expr="{ match: 'settles', marker: 'claude' }"/>

    <!--
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT ACTUALLY KILLS A RUN OF THIS KIND — register item 738, layer 1,
      and the layer that had cost real work rather than only real typing.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ MEASURED IN THIS DAEMON'S OWN RUN REGISTRY, 2026-08-28, and the
      measurement is the whole argument. `state/sprag/sprag-loop.runs.json` holds
      **49 runs**:

        exhausted (cost)         **8**   every one at 65,809 to 68,658 bytes
        exhausted (iterations)   4
        the largest run that CONVERGED   run 17: 1,231 iterations, 516,020 bytes
        next four by spend       505,759 (38) - 362,422 (29) - 340,343 (39) - 215,328 (19)

      ⇒ **ONE RUN IN SIX WAS KILLED BY A NUMBER NOBODY CHOSE.** The daemon's
      `DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES` is 64 KiB, and a debt run that finishes its work spends
      HALF A MEGABYTE — eight times that. So the default is not a backstop for
      this kind, it is the ceiling that bites first, and it bites mid-round with
      the work uncommitted in the tree.

      ⚠⚠ AND THE PEER'S REPORT WAS ABOUT A RUN IN THIS FILE. The item was filed
      from another repository's watcher quoting *"run 41, exhausted (cost) after
      49 iterations, 65809 bytes"*. Run 41 of THIS registry is
      `exhausted / cost / 49 / 65809`. One daemon drives all four loops, so the
      evidence was never somebody else's — it was here, unread.

      ⛔ WHAT WAS DEFENDING AGAINST IT: a bash `if` chain in an untracked launcher
      script, refusing any launch that did not name all three ceilings by hand.
      That is the thing the owner rejected and the reason this item exists — a
      copy of somebody's memory is not a spec. These three clauses are the spec.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE KEYS ARE THE WIRE'S OWN, and the reader checks them against the
      PUBLICATION (`PluginGrammar::guardrail_fields`) rather than a list of its
      own, so a key this document spells that no guardrail admits is REFUSED
      naming what it takes. An unclassified key is not a pass — it is the whole
      failure mode `parse_guardrails` already refuses on the caller's side, and a
      document silently granted the looser bound would be worse.
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ **max_bytes — `never`, BY THE OWNER'S DECISION OF 2026-09-07.**
      Register item 941.

      It read TWO MEBIBYTES and was argued this way: *four times the largest run
      this daemon has ever recorded converging (516,020), and at that run's own
      measured rate of 419 bytes per iteration about five thousand iterations of
      typing — a real bound rather than a formality, because a loop that has
      stopped making progress and is still typing meets it.*

      ⚠⚠⚠ THAT ARGUMENT IS STILL TRUE AND THE OWNER TOOK THE TRADE ANYWAY. The
      question put was whether to lift the clock alone or all three, and the
      answer was all three. **So this clause loses a rail rather than a
      formality**, and that is written here rather than in a summary nobody
      re-reads: with the two spending bounds gone, nothing left in `Ceiling::ALL`
      measures PROGRESS — the four remaining endings (converged, blocked,
      unadmitted, stood_down) all need the loop to judge itself or a person to
      step in.

      ⛔ WHAT TO DO IF THAT COSTS SOMETHING, and it is deliberately NOT to put a
      number back: build a ceiling that measures progress — *N steps with no
      commit and no ledger edit* — which is the predicate `max_bytes` was standing
      in for all along. Do not build it before a stopped run is actually observed;
      an unmeasured number is what this whole clause refuses.

      ⚠ AND THE STEP CEILING BELOW STILL HOLDS A NUMBER, which is not a second
      decision: `Guardrails::max_iterations` is a `u32` and has no value meaning
      *no bound*. Giving it one is this item's second layer.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ **max_iterations — `never`, AND WITH IT ALL THREE.** Register item
      941, second layer.

      It read FIVE THOUSAND and was argued as *agreeing* with the byte ceiling
      rather than being a second guess: the largest converged run took 1,231
      steps, five thousand is four times that, and 5,000 x 419 bytes is 2.1 MB, so
      the two bit at about the same place. **That agreement was the argument**,
      and it is why they leave together: with `max_bytes` gone this number had
      nothing left to agree with, and a lone step ceiling of five thousand would
      be exactly the *decorative bound* the sentence warned about.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THIS ONE NEEDED THE SUBSTRATE TO CHANGE. Its two neighbours were
      already `Option` in `Guardrails`; this was a `u32`, so *no bound* was a
      thing the daemon could not carry and it REFUSED the word for one commit,
      naming the reason. Giving the field a value for *none* touched 147 places
      the compiler listed, and that is why it is a second commit rather than a
      wider first one.

      ⛔ SO THIS KIND'S RUNS NOW END ONLY ON WHAT THEY JUDGE THEMSELVES —
      converged, blocked, unadmitted, stood_down — or on a person. That is the
      owner's decision of 2026-09-07 and the residue is written under `max_bytes`
      above: if it costs something, build a ceiling that measures PROGRESS, not a
      number put back here.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ **max_seconds — `never`, AND THE RE-MEASURE THIS CLAUSE ASKED FOR
      IS WHAT PUT IT THERE.** Register item 941.

      The clause used to read SIX HOURS and said of itself: *"it is the one number
      here nothing measured … the day a run is cut by `duration` with work
      uncommitted, this is the clause to re-measure."* That day came twice (runs
      245 and 250), and the re-measure convicted the number by THIS DOCUMENT'S
      OWN TEST — the one written three paragraphs up: *two bounds that fire far
      apart mean one of them is decorative.* Measured on run 250 (278 iterations,
      120,227 bytes, 8.8 hours):

        max_bytes       2 MiB   at 432 B/iteration   about 4,854 iterations
        max_iterations  5000    at 278 per 8.8 h     about 158 hours (6.6 days)
        max_seconds     21600                        **26 times sooner**

      ⇒ So the decorative bounds were the two that AGREE, and the thing actually
      ending every run was the one nothing measured. The owner's instruction of
      2026-09-07 — every sprag loop unbounded — is the decision; this word is how
      the document says it.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THE WORD AND NOT A LARGE NUMBER. A week in seconds would be a
      number nobody reasoned about, which is precisely what the paragraph above
      refuses, and a reader told `604800` learns nothing while a reader told
      `never` learns the whole of it. The word is not new here: `max_turns` below
      declines its count the same way, and so does `reaim_max`.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT STILL ENDS A RUN, so that *unbounded* is not read as *unstoppable*:
      the two bounds above, and they are now the whole of it. A run that has
      stopped making progress and is still typing meets them at about 4,854
      iterations — which is the axis this loop should be bounded on, because a
      loop that is working should not be cut for having worked a long time. ⛔ The
      residue, stated: nothing here bounds a run that makes SLOW progress
      indefinitely, and the rail against that is a person reading the round
      summaries, not a clock.

      ⚠ AND A CALLER MAY STILL NAME A NUMBER, per-field, which is register item
      300 — what changed is that a caller who names NOTHING now gets what this
      document decided instead of this daemon's six-hour constant.
    -->
    <data id="guardrails"
          expr="{
            max_bytes:      'never',
            max_iterations: 'never',
            max_seconds:    'never'
          }"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW LONG A PERSON MAY HOLD A RUN OF THIS KIND — SIX HOURS, AND SINCE
      REGISTER ITEM 941 IT IS ON ITS OWN AXIS RATHER THAN DERIVED FROM THE CLAUSE
      ABOVE.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY IT IS HERE AT ALL, WHICH IS THE GATE'S DOING. `Ceiling::ALL` names
      five things that can end a run, and the gate register item 738 built walks
      that set and asks THIS DOCUMENT for the bound each one fires on. There is no
      exemption arm: a ceiling nobody classified is a RED, not a pass. `Hold` was
      the fifth, and leaving it to the template would have been exactly the escape
      hatch that disarms the gate.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE OLD ARGUMENT IS GONE, AND IT IS WORTH SAYING WHAT IT WAS. This
      number read *equal to `max_seconds`*, because **a hold longer than the run's
      own wall-clock budget can never be handed back to a live run** — equal was
      the largest value that was not a lie. `max_seconds` is `never` now, so every
      hold is handable back and that sentence bounds nothing. Keeping the sentence
      would have left a derivation pointing at a clause that no longer exists,
      which is the shape register item 933 keeps arriving as.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO WHAT IS THIS NUMBER ABOUT. It is the only bound left between a run
      somebody took and a person who did not come back — a question about PEOPLE,
      not about runs, and a different axis from the one above. **NOTHING HERE
      MEASURES IT**: read 2026-09-07 across every run registry on this machine,
      no run has ever ended on `hold` at all (`ceiling` was `turns` once and
      `duration` once). So it is stated rather than derived, on `context_ceiling`'s
      own terms, and six hours is kept because it is the number this document has
      carried — changing it would invent a second unmeasured one.

      ⛔ AND IT MUST NOT BECOME `never` TOO, which is the tempting next move now
      that the run is untimed: a hold that never expires is a pane this loop can
      never have back. A gate in `kind.rs` asserts exactly that pair — the run
      declines its clock AND this stays finite.

      ⚠ MILLISECONDS, because that is the unit the id carries. Six hours is
      21,600,000.
    -->
    <data id="hold_within_ms" expr="21600000"/>

    <!--
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ HOW MANY STEPS A RUN OF THIS KIND MAY TAKE WITHOUT GETTING
      ANYWHERE — register item 942, and the rail the clause under `max_bytes`
      above said to build if declining all three guardrails cost something.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ IT COST SOMETHING THE SAME DAY. That clause reads: *"with the two
      spending bounds gone, nothing left in `Ceiling::ALL` measures PROGRESS"*,
      and it named the repair — *build a ceiling that measures progress, N steps
      with no commit and no ledger edit, not a number put back here.* This is
      that ceiling. The number below is derived, and the derivation is written
      beside it because the clause it replaces was convicted for not having one.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE DERIVATION, RE-RUN OVER EVERY RUN LOG THIS DAEMON HOLDS
      (2026-09-07; 241 logs that hold a walk, **60 of them runs that CONVERGED**
      — the same population `max_bytes` was derived on, and named here rather
      than chosen afterwards). ⚠ THE POPULATION IS THE SIXTY, not the 241: a
      live daemon writes another log while anybody is reading this, so the file
      count is a moving number and was one WITHIN THIS ROUND (240, then 241).
      What the bound rests on is the converged set and the stretch below. One timestamped journal line is one driver step to within
      **2.4%**, checked against the run registry's own `iterations` on nine runs
      — the worst is run 245, 126 lines for 123 steps, and the excess is header
      lines, so a bound derived in lines errs slightly LARGE, which is the safe
      direction for a ceiling. For each step, *did a mark move in the interval
      since the previous step*, taken from this repository's git history:

        predicate                              longest stretch, converged runs
        no milestone claimed                       1197 steps   (run 17)
        no COMMIT landed                            325 steps   (run 17)
        NEITHER                                     325 steps   (run 17)

      ⇒ **325 x 4 = 1300**, four times the largest stretch a run that finished
      its work has ever actually passed through — the same multiplier and the
      same anchor `max_bytes` used, which took *"four times the largest run this
      daemon has ever recorded converging"* and landed on run 17 as well.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE MIDDLE ROW SAID *no mark moved* AND THAT WAS A WIDER CLAIM
      THAN THE MEASUREMENT — register item 946, and the row is now spelled as
      what it is. The predicate is taken from THIS REPOSITORY'S GIT HISTORY, one
      paragraph up, and git history is the REFLOG mark alone: the register is
      deliberately not in git (the clause below this one argues why), so it was
      never in the population and its own stretches are not measured here.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THAT IS WHY THIS NUMBER SURVIVES A MARK GOING UNREADABLE, which
      is the question item 946 was opened to ask. **325 is a ONE-TERM
      measurement**, so 1300 is already four times what a single-signal axis
      needs. A run that can read both marks is bounded by the AND of them, and a
      stretch where NEITHER moved is a SUBSET of one where the reflog did not —
      so full arity carries slack ABOVE this derivation, and a run reduced to
      the reflog alone is back on exactly the footing the number was derived on.
      A gate in `kind.rs` asserts that floor and asks it about every arity this
      list can degrade to, so the number cannot quietly fall below its own
      derivation and nobody can make the floor scale with the count.

      ⚠⚠ THE RESIDUE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the OTHER degradation is not
      measured. If the reflog is the mark that stops answering, the surviving
      term is the register, and the register cannot be measured backwards at all
      because it is not versioned. Rule (8) of the standing rules a run of this
      kind is given — *round end = commit + ledger update + push* — puts a
      register write beside every commit, so its stretches ought to be the
      shorter of the two. That is a rule and not a measurement, and item 946
      closes with it stated rather than proven.

      ⚠ RE-DERIVED 2026-09-07 by the round that closed item 946, over the same
      population and independently: 60 converged runs (one RUN-ENDED line each —
      run231's log holds TWO of them), 2,050 commits on record, and both numbers
      above came back to the digit. Run 17 is 1,231 steps by its own registry
      and by a line count that agrees exactly.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND WHAT THE DERIVATION REFUTED, recorded because the register asked
      for the re-run rather than the answer. The item proposed 48, from a sample
      of four runs whose longest stretch was 12 REFLECTIONS. Two things were
      wrong with it. The command it derived from drops the stretch AFTER the last
      milestone, so a run that banked nothing at all reports its longest stretch
      as ZERO — measured, run 142 reports 0 where the true answer is 18, and 35
      of the 170 runs with reflections bank nothing. And four runs is not the
      population: over all of them the same predicate reaches 29 reflections, not
      12. The unit moved too, for the reason `stall_after_steps` states.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT THIS IS NOT, so *unbounded* stays true where 941 left it: it is not
      a clock and not a spend. A run that works for a week and commits is never
      touched by it, which is the property the owner's instruction of 2026-09-07
      asked for and the one a wall clock could not give. What it ends is a run
      that is still typing while nothing it is supposed to move has moved.

      ⛔ AND IT MUST NOT BECOME `never`, which is the tempting move the day it
      fires on something inconvenient: this is now the ONLY ceiling of the five
      this kind admits that measures progress at all, and declining it would put
      this document back where item 942 found it. A gate in `kind.rs` asserts
      exactly that pair — the run declines its three guardrails AND this stays a
      number.
    -->
    <data id="stall_after_steps" expr="1300"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHAT A ROUND OF THIS KIND MOVES WHEN IT IS GETTING SOMEWHERE —
      the other half of the bound above, and the half that keeps its predicate
      from being one thing. Register item 942.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THESE TWO ARE THIS REPOSITORY'S DEFINITION OF A ROUND, and they are
      not a guess: the standing rules a run of this kind is given say *round end
      = commit + ledger update + push*, so a round that has got anywhere has
      moved BOTH of these files and one that has got nowhere has moved neither.

        `.git/logs/HEAD`  the reflog, appended by every commit and every ref this
                          tree moves — MEASURED rather than assumed, 2026-09-07:
                          **2,041** of its lines are `commit` entries against
                          **2,043** commits on `main` (the two older than the
                          reflog itself), and 92 further lines are the other ref
                          movements. Relative, so it resolves against whatever
                          checkout the run's pane was opened in — the same read
                          `works_in` above takes, and the only spelling that is
                          true in every checkout this document is compiled into.
                          ⚠ NOT `.git/refs/heads/main`: a packed ref is not a
                          loose file, and a mark that vanishes when somebody runs
                          `git gc` is a mark that reads as *nothing ever moves*.

        the register       absolute, and named absolutely for `successor_check`'s
                          own stated reason one clause up: this path is this
                          repository's, and a mark resolved against whatever tree
                          a run happened to land in would answer about somebody
                          else's work. It is the SAME file `successor_check`
                          reads and `reference` points a run at.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THE REGISTER IS A MARK AT ALL, when a commit is the durable act:
      the register is not in git. A round that has judged an item, written its
      measurement and not yet committed has done real work that no commit shows,
      and a bound that could not see it would count that round as stalled.

      ⚠⚠ THE RESIDUE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: this tree has TWO WRITERS
      (register item 196 — a person's session and the loop share it), so a commit
      made by the OTHER writer clears this run's stall count. That is the same
      residue the item's own proposal (a `git log` window) carries, and it fails
      in the safe direction: it can only make this ceiling fire LATER, never
      sooner, and the bound is four times the largest stretch anything has needed.
    -->
    <data id="progress_marks"
          expr="[
            '.git/logs/HEAD',
            '/home/coin/.claude/projects/-home-coin-sprag/memory/debt-open.md'
          ]"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHERE A CHECKER OF THIS KIND SHOULD START LOOKING — register item
      1074, and a DIFFERENT list from the two marks above on purpose.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE MARKS GAVE A CHECKER, AND WHY THAT IS THE ITEM. Item 1072
      put the files to open into the question — the one remedy anybody has TIMED
      for a checker going silent — and fed it from `progress_marks`, because a
      document had no other list. Run that list through the driver's own
      reduction and THIS kind's checker was handed exactly one path: the
      register is outside the tree and cannot be named to a judge standing in a
      copy, leaving `.git/logs/HEAD`. A reflog says a commit happened and
      nothing whatever about what was fixed. The measured arm was switched on
      with nothing to feed it.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHY DIRECTORIES AND NOT FILES, which is the honest shape for this
      kind. A round of this loop pays ONE register item, and which files that
      touches is different every round — a static list of files would be wrong
      on almost every run, and a judge told to open the wrong five files is
      worse off than one told to search. What IS stable is where the work lands,
      and that is a measurement rather than a guess: over the 200 commits before
      2026-09-12 (702 changed paths), every directory taking at least a
      TWENTIETH of them is named below and nothing else is. The five hold
      **80.1%** of the changed paths while being 196 of the tree's 367 tracked
      files, so a judge that opens these and stops has missed a fifth of what a
      round of this kind has ever moved.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE THRESHOLD IS WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES
      THIS LIST RE-DERIVABLE. A list chosen by eye is a list nobody can check
      and nobody can update. The word *a twentieth* is what the next reader
      measures against, the exact command that produces the table is in the doc
      of `the_debt_kind_sends_a_checker_somewhere_other_than_its_own_reflog`
      (an XML comment cannot hold a git flag), and that gate is also what
      refuses a name here that has stopped existing.

      ⚠⚠ THE REGISTER IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, though it is a mark above and
      though a round writes in it. The checker stands in a COPY and mutates what
      it judges (item 705), so a path outside the tree may never be named to it
      — the driver would drop this one anyway, and writing it here would be a
      sentence claiming something the product refuses. What a run says to
      CONSULT is `reference` below, which is the clause that world belongs to.

      ⚠ AND `.git/logs/HEAD` IS ABSENT for the opposite reason: it is a perfect
      stall mark and worthless to a judge. That the two lists barely overlap is
      the item, not an oversight.
    -->
    <data id="check_opens"
          expr="[
            'crates/sprag-plugin/src',
            'crates/sprag-host/src',
            'crates/sprag-gate/src',
            'crates/sprag-gate/tests',
            'crates/sprag-host/tests'
          ]"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHERE A RUN OF THIS KIND WORKS — register item 738, layer 4, and the
      one fact about a debt run that nothing anywhere had ever written down.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS WHEN NOBODY SAYS IT, measured 2026-08-25 by the owner
      asking why the same screen kept coming back. A pane opened without a
      directory stands in `$HOME`, and `claude` starting there asks

          Accessing workspace: /home/coin
          Quick safety check: Is this a project you created or one you trust?
          1. Yes, I trust this folder     2. No, exit

      — a dialog this loop CANNOT answer, because `may_answer` covers editing and
      running commands and nothing else. The run stopped there and waited for a
      person who was not watching. In one reading of one daemon: `inner-wz` was
      `blocked rule=dialog-choice-list` in `/home/coin` while three sibling panes
      standing in their own repositories were all `working`. The control and the
      symptom came out of the SAME restart, which is what made it a measurement
      rather than a story about how `claude` behaves.

      ⛔ AND THE WRONG REPAIR IS NAMED, because it is the obvious one: adding this
      dialog to `may_answer` AUTOMATES A FALSE ANSWER. `Yes, I trust this folder`
      consents to `/home/coin` — every repository on the machine — while the true
      fact is narrower and duller: *this pane is not standing in the tree this run
      is about*. A loop that says the true thing needs to know the tree, and until
      this clause there was nowhere for that to be written.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO WHAT THIS BUYS IS A REFUSAL, NOT AN ANSWER. The door asks whether the
      directory the pane was opened in carries this marker and declines to start a
      run that would walk into that dialog — naming the directory and the marker,
      so the remedy (reopen the pane with `-c`) is in the sentence. **Refusing
      before the run is the only point where anybody is still watching**; item
      684's whole cost was a run that had already been started and had nobody left
      to tell.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ AND IT IS A MARKER, NOT A PATH — WHICH THE TESTS REFUTED IN THIS
      ROUND RATHER THAN A READER PREDICTING IT. The first form of this clause read
      `'/home/coin/sprag'`, and **nineteen gates went red at once**: the build
      machine's checkout of this repository is `/home/developer/remote-build/sprag`
      and its fixtures' panes stand somewhere else again. **The same document is
      compiled into every checkout there will ever be**, so a path in it names ONE
      machine and is false everywhere else — including the machine that runs the
      gate that would defend it.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE CLAUSE ANSWERS THE QUESTION THAT IS ACTUALLY MACHINE-INDEPENDENT:
      **what makes a directory the tree this kind works in.** A repository carries
      `.git`; a home directory does not. That predicate separates the measured
      symptom from the measured control exactly — `/home/coin` fails, and all four
      of `sprag`·`pinion`·`watching-zenoh`·`scxml-core-engine` pass — and it is the
      same NEEDLE idiom every other clause in this file already uses.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS `exists`, NOT `is_dir`: a linked worktree carries `.git` as a FILE,
      and a check that demanded a directory would refuse a tree that is perfectly
      real. ⚠ The residue, stated: a checkout with no `.git` at all — an export, a
      tarball — is refused, and that is the direction to be wrong in.

      ⚠ AND IT IS ASKED OF THE PANE'S OWN RECORD OF WHERE IT WAS OPENED
      (`PaneOrigin::pane_start_dir`), never of `/proc/<pid>/cwd`. That distinction
      IS item 684's first half: the kernel reading answers `None` the moment the
      child exits, and a replacement asked for at exactly that moment is how the
      wrong directory used to be inherited for ever. ⚠⚠ It is also the SAME read
      item 710 already takes as *the run's repository* when it stands the milestone
      checker up — so this clause is what finally CHECKS a fact that door has been
      assuming since it was built.
    -->
    <data id="works_in" expr="'.git'"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHICH WINDOW A RUN OF THIS KIND STANDS IN — register item 754, and
      the clause `works_in` above is one half of. That one says which TREE a run's
      pane must be opened in; this one says whose SCREEN it may appear on, and
      nothing anywhere had ever written it down either.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS WHEN NOBODY SAYS IT, measured 2026-08-29 by the owner
      looking at the screen and asking *"pinion 에 pane 이 세 개 있는데, 이게
      보이는 화면에 붙이려다보니까 생기는 버그 아니야?"*. The reading:

          windows:  sprag · guilaunch · pinion (current) · wz · sce
          panes in the CURRENT window (= pinion):
            6:   outer-pinion
            308: inner750        ← THIS repository's loop, in pinion's window
            318: inner-pinion

      A request that narrows no window acts in the session's CURRENT one, so a
      pane is born wherever a person happens to be looking. This loop's watcher
      had called `split-window` more than ten times over that day and named no
      window once, and the panes went to three different windows — every call
      reporting success, because from the daemon's side every one of them was.

      ⛔ AND THE WRONG REPAIR IS NAMED, because it is the obvious one and the owner
      refused it in words: *"결정론적으로 이런 문제가 절대 일어나면 안 돼,
      컨텍스트나 메모리에 의존해서는 안 돼."* **A watcher that remembers to pass
      the window is not a mechanism** — it is a rule held in whoever is on shift,
      and this register has the same sentence about `-c`, about the three
      ceilings, and about the reference. A rule that spreads between watchers is a
      symptom, not a cure.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO WHAT THIS BUYS IS A REFUSAL, on `works_in`'s exact terms: the door
      asks whether the window the pane stands in belongs to the tree this run
      works in, and declines a run whose pane landed in somebody else's work. The
      birth side is answered separately and structurally — `sprag split-window`
      takes `-w`, and with neither `-w` nor a pane it now stands where its CALLER
      stands rather than where a person is looking — so this clause is what
      notices when that is bypassed, at the last moment anybody is watching.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ AND IT IS A PREDICATE, NOT A WINDOW NAME, for the reason the clause
      above records in the same words: **the same document is compiled into every
      checkout there will ever be**, and the four repositories that share this
      daemon call their windows `sprag`, `pinion`, `wz`, `sce` while their trees
      are named otherwise. A name here would be true in one window of one daemon
      and false everywhere else, INCLUDING the fixture that defends it.

      ⚠⚠⚠ SO THE CLAUSE ANSWERS THE QUESTION THAT IS ACTUALLY PORTABLE: *a window
      belongs to one tree*. A run stands in a window whose other panes were opened
      in the same tree it works in, and never in one already holding somebody
      else's. That predicate separates the measured symptom from the measured
      control exactly — `inner750` (a `sprag` pane among `pinion`'s) fails, and
      `inner-sprag`, `inner-pinion`, `inner-wz`, `inner-sce` all pass — with no
      opinion at all about what a window is CALLED.

      ⚠⚠ A PANE THAT NAMES NO TREE CONTRADICTS NOTHING. The watcher's own shell is
      born in a home directory and the GUI's pane in nowhere in particular; those
      are absences of a claim, not claims of a different tree, which is the same
      reading `works_in`'s `None` gets one clause up. What this refuses is a
      window where TWO trees are named, because that is the state the owner was
      looking at.

      ⚠ AND IT IS ASKED OF EACH PANE'S OWN RECORD OF WHERE IT WAS OPENED
      (`PaneOrigin::pane_start_dir`), never of `/proc/<pid>/cwd` — item 684's
      first half, and the same read `works_in` takes.
    -->
    <data id="stands_in" expr="'tree'"/>

    <!--
      WHICH DIMENSION A RUN OF THIS KIND MUST KEEP WHOLE — register item 772,
      and `stands_in`'s sibling one axis in.

      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE OWNER ASKED, LOOKING AT THE SCREEN (2026-08-30):
      *"어떤건 세로로 split 됐고 어떤건 가로로 split 되어있어, 결정론적이여야
      되는거아니야?"* — and the answer was no. Four windows of one daemon, four
      loops of this kind, and the direction each was split in was whatever its
      watcher had typed.

          sprag   left|right   inner 73 rows x 168 cols   ->  765's budget 67 rows
          pinion  top|bottom   inner 36 rows x 338 cols   ->  765's budget 30 rows
          wz      left|right   inner 73 rows x 169 cols   ->  765's budget 67 rows
          sce     left|right   inner 73 rows x 169 cols   ->  765's budget 67 rows

      ⇒ ONE OF THE FOUR HAD HALF THE BUDGET, and nobody had decided that. The
      two directions bite opposite things: `left|right` halves the WIDTH (the
      delivery-confirmation axis, where a composer folding a paste is item 788's
      subject) and `top|bottom` halves the HEIGHT — which is item 765's axis,
      because a reply's FIRST row is its label and the first thing scrolling
      takes. A loop that must read its own answers cannot afford the second.

      ⛔ AND THE ROOT WAS WORSE THAN A HABIT. pinion's launch procedure had
      `split-window -v` FROZEN INTO IT while the other three carried `-h`; the
      skill spells `-h` as an EXAMPLE and says nowhere why. So it was not a
      choice anybody made per run — it was one character carried forward in a
      brief, with no ground to change it on. Telling a watcher *use `-h`* does
      not reach a brief that already says `-v`, which is precisely why this is
      the document's to say (item 738's conclusion, item 754's shape).

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND IT IS A DIMENSION, NOT A DIRECTION, for the reason `stands_in`
      is a predicate and not a window name: the same document is compiled into
      every checkout. `-h` is a spelling of one launcher's grammar; *this kind
      keeps its rows* is the fact behind it, true of a run driven by any surface
      — and it is the one a door can check against a pane it did not open.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT IT DOES NOT SAY, stated rather than implied. This fixes the axis a
      watcher CHOOSES. It does not fix the axis a CLIENT moves: register item
      772 also measured four panes dragged to a new geometry by a GUI resize
      with nobody touching them (`window-size latest`), and no value here can
      refuse that — it is not a decision, it is a client reporting its size.
      That half stays open, and this clause is not to be read as closing it.

      ⚠ `'rows'` is the only word this build knows. An unknown one is a REFUSAL
      and not a pass, the same as `stands_in`'s — a rule nothing can apply is
      not a rule a run may be started under.
    -->
    <data id="keeps" expr="'rows'"/>
  </datamodel>

  <!--
    Nothing happens here. The datamodel above IS this document, and the driver
    reads it without ever stepping the machine — see the header for why a kind
    with real steps would not change this file's shape but replace it with one
    the driver drives.

    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHICH IS WHY THIS DOCUMENT ANSWERS NO ERROR OF ITS OWN, AND SAYS SO
    HERE RATHER THAN LEAVING A READER TO WONDER — register item 505.

    Every `<data>` above is an expression, and W3C SCXML 5.2/5.3 raises
    `error.execution` when one of them cannot be evaluated. W3C 3.12.2 then
    IGNORES that event if nothing matches it, and a document whose only state is
    `<final>` has nowhere a transition could live: a final state takes none, and
    there is no other. So a broken clause here would leave the driver reading
    whatever the datamodel happened to hold — a consent list that came out empty,
    a standing instruction that is not there — and a run would proceed under
    decisions its author did not make.

    ⚠⚠⚠ THE ANSWER IS THEREFORE THE DOOR'S, and it exists: `sprag_plugin::
    document::opened` initialises every driven document and REFUSES one whose
    start-up raised an error nobody answered, so a kind that cannot evaluate its
    own clauses stops the run before it starts instead of quietly weakening it.
    `LoopKind::debt` is opened through it, and `NoKind::Faulted` is the sentence
    a person reads.

    ⚠ It is the honest split rather than a shortcut: what to DO about a failure
    is a decision, and this document has no state in which to take one. The
    template, which has fourteen, answers its own errors on the `work` region.
  -->
  <final id="decided"/>
</scxml>
